Below is the Mandelbrot set rendered as characters, computed in C compiled to
`wasm32` — no emscripten, no libc, no toolchain past `clang` and `wasm-ld`.
**1.4KB** of wasm.

Press **space** to run the zoom, **r** to reset. Click it first so it has focus.

## how the bytes cross over

The C exports a `char*` into its own linear memory. On the JS side that memory
is an `ArrayBuffer`, so the buffer becomes a `Uint8Array` view at that offset
and a `TextDecoder` turns it into a string. Nothing is copied until we ask; no
allocator is involved on either side.

    char *buffer(void) { return buf; }
    void  render(double t);

That is the whole interface. Twelve lines of glue in `/js/wasm.js`.

## no libm

Freestanding means no `sin`, no `pow`, no `memset` — clang still *emits* calls
to `memset` for zero-init, so the module supplies its own four-line version or
the link fails. The zoom is a loop of multiplications rather than `pow()` for
the same reason. Escape-time fractals are a good fit precisely because they
need nothing but arithmetic.

> The centre is a point on the boundary, so the zoom never runs out of
> structure. It will keep finding detail until the doubles do.

See also [[garden-of-life]] and [[crossword]].
